{"records":[{"id":"5w9pvndgrf","title":"Ca2+ is an intracellular danger signal promoting STING activation","description":"Calcium release in host cells is essential for viral infection and survival, and mutations disrupting calcium homeostasis are associated with chronic viral infections or autoimmune diseases in patients, yet the biological significance of Ca2+ signaling on STING activation remains unclear. Here we show that cytosolic Ca2+ is sensed by host cells as an immediate-early danger signal. Viral infection-activated Rho GTPases and phospholipase C-β induce ITPR-mediated, while STING translocation induces TRPV2-mediated ER Ca2+ release, both leading to store-operated Ca2+ entry through ORAI channels. Ca2+ influx activates calmodulin (CaM)-dependent protein kinases CaMKK and CaMK1D. Activated CaMK1D phosphorylates STING allowing for TBK1 trans-autophosphorylation. Inhibiting Ca2+ signaling or mutating CaMK1D-phosphorylating STING sites impairs STING activation. Ca2+ signaling is also required for STING-mediated autophagy and lysosome biogenesis, inhibited by CaMKK or CaMK inhibitors, or by deleting CaMKK1/2 or CaMK1D. These results support a two-signal mode for STING activation in which the Ca2+-CaM-CaMKK-CaMK cascade is indispensable.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-15T15:35:17.628Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5w9pvndgrf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5w9pvndgrf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nanhai","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Han","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xurui","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanyuan","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanhao","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yilin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Run","last_name":"Fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chenguang","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luhua","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Phosphorylation","Autophagy","Innate Immunity","Calcium","Phospholipase C","cGAS-STING Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"66ms37y66x","title":"Data for: A genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen reveals that riboflavin regulates hydrogen peroxide entry into HAP1 cells  ","description":"CRISPR screen data for hydrogen peroxide mediated cell death","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-13T14:30:16.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66ms37y66x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66ms37y66x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Mark","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamutenda","last_name":"Chidawanyika","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oxidative Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"txnvtdw2zs","title":"GPI-Anchored Ligand-BioID2-Tagging System Identifies Galectin-1 Mediating Zika Virus Entry Gao et al","description":"Identification of host factors facilitating pathogen entry is critical for preventing infectious diseases. Here, we report a tagging system consisting of a viral receptor-binding protein (RBP) linked to BioID2, which is expressed on the cell surface via a GPI anchor. Using VSV or Zika virus (ZIKV) RBP, the system (BioID2- RBP(V)-GPI; BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI) faithfully identifies LDLR and AXL, the receptors of VSV and ZIKV, respectively. Being GPI-anchored is essential for the probe to function properly. Furthermore, BioID2-RBP(Z)-GPI expressed in human neuronal progenitor cells identifies galectin-1 on cell surface pivotal for ZIKV entry. This conclusion is further supported by antibody blocking and galectin-1 silencing in A549 and mouse neural cells. Importantly, Lgas1-/- mice are significantly more resistant to ZIKV infection than Lgas1+/+ littermates are, having significantly lower virus titers and fewer pathologies in various organs. This tagging system may have broad applications for identifying protein-protein interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T19:31:31.48Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/txnvtdw2zs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/txnvtdw2zs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shanshan","last_name":"Gao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jzyp2w6hzt","title":"Source data for \"Periodicity, Mixed-Mode Oscillations, and Multiple Timescale in a phosphoinositide-Rho GTPase Network","description":"Source data for the plots made in \"Periodicity, Mixed-Mode Oscillations, and Multiple Timescale in a phosphoinositide-Rho GTPase Network.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-23T06:03:27.661Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jzyp2w6hzt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jzyp2w6hzt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"CHEE SAN","last_name":"TONG","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Wu","orcid":"0000-0002-9161-7940"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Chaos Theory","Phosphoinositides","Membrane","Oscillation","Contractility"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9gdnp4wh99","title":"Source data for 'Periodicity, Mixed-Mode Oscillations, and Multiple Timescale in a Phosphoinositide-Rho GTPase Network' ","description":"Source data for used for figures in Tong et al, 2023, Cell Reports.\nPeriodicity, Mixed-Mode Oscillations, and Multiple Timescale in a Phosphoinositide-Rho GTPase Network","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-20T06:06:30.827Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9gdnp4wh99","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9gdnp4wh99"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"CHEE SAN","last_name":"TONG","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Wu","orcid":"0000-0002-9161-7940"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Chaos Theory","Phosphoinositides","Membrane","Oscillation","Contractility"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cdgx63gd5y","title":"TRIM21 induces selective autophagy of viruses and bacteria","description":"TRIM21 is an exceptionally versatile ubiquitin ligase that can be directed by antibodies to target oligomeric protein scaffolds, viral capsids, and proteopathic aggregates for intracellular degradation. How the cell degrades these typically resistant substrates remains poorly understood. To address this, we used TRIM21 viral restriction to create a genome-wide phenotypic screen for antibody-dependent capsid degradation. We identify an antimicrobial selective macroautophagy pathway in mammalian cells, which we term \"antibody-directed xenophagy.\" We show this mechanism restricts structurally diverse pathogens, including adenovirus and Salmonella. Using quantitative microscopy, we demonstrate that TRIM21 rapidly intercepts antibody-pathogen complexes, leading to ubiquitin ligase activation. Following this, selective autophagy adaptors are recruited and viral cargoes are delivered to lysosomes. This process reduces Salmonella pathology and bacterial tissue invasion in mice. We propose that TRIM21 evolved through competition with pathogens to induce autophagy of diverse and complex substrates, potentially explaining its versatility for targeted protein degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-29T20:32:28.807Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cdgx63gd5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cdgx63gd5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"Rhinesmith","orcid":"0000-0002-9983-0287"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"U105181010"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"223054/Z/21/Z"}],"categories":["Protein Degradation","Autophagy","Host Immune Response","Antiviral Immunity","Bacterial Host Response","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":89505,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":116,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":89505,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":89234,"restricted":52,"embargoed":219},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1404},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":469},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":337},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":318},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":309},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":305},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":269},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":217},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":175},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":175}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":87188},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1764},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":156},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":90},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":62},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":51},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":23}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"0ad